INSERT INTO `courses_lesson` VALUES (3,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Mathematics Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',1,2,NULL),(4,'Introduction: About Real Numbers  , Division Algorithm','video','https://youtu.be/SDFmwzZgT2c?si=PbLgtuEhbEaYpwpE','','### . Introduction: About Real Numbers  \nIn this section, we learn about **real numbers**, which include natural numbers, whole numbers, integers, rational numbers, and irrational numbers. They form the basis of almost every concept in mathematics.\n###  Division Algorithm  \nHere, we study the **Division Algorithm** which states that for any integers *a* and *b* (b ≠ 0), we can write:  \n**a = bq + r**, where 0 ≤ r < b.  \nThis is useful in solving problems related to divisibility.',0,2,NULL),(5,'Euclid\'s algorithm && LCM and HCF','video','https://www.youtube.com/live/IQE9OMTYy_A?si=4Eurl0C-C5eVkWhR&t=185','','### Euclid\'s Algorithm & LCM and HCF  \nThis section introduces **Euclid’s Division Lemma** and shows how it is used to find the **HCF of two numbers**.  \nWe also understand the relationship between **HCF and LCM**.\n\n---\n',1,2,NULL),(6,'Exercise 1.1 & Examples in Text Book Problems and Concept','video','    https://youtu.be/MwfoHi_BcFc?si=G3mOO87SrP3VCDDT','','## Exercise 1.1 & Examples  \nPractice questions and solved examples related to **Euclid’s Division Lemma, HCF, and LCM**.',2,2,NULL),(7,'The Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic','video',' https://youtu.be/DSTdc8n6FDM?si=-6zu1KkeA_O6VMPD','','###  Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic  \nThis theorem states that **every composite number can be uniquely expressed as a product of prime numbers** (ignoring the order).  \nIt helps in factorization and solving divisibility problems.',3,2,NULL),(8,'Exercise 1.2 : 1,2 Problems and Concepts','video','    https://youtu.be/eyJN1c5vOVs?si=MXzLc1CZFnl46djx','','###  Exercise 1.2 & Examples  \nProblems based on **prime factorization and Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic**.\n',4,2,NULL),(9,'Exercise 1.2: 3,4 and 5,6 Problems and Concepts','video','     https://youtu.be/i1ji9fYv7sc?si=666MIUNOT7uu-Q5w','','###  Exercise 1.2 & Examples  \nProblems based on **prime factorization and Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic**.\n',5,2,NULL),(10,'Real Number  and Their Decimal Expansions','video','   https://youtu.be/7MP813rgaaQ?si=B4y1aFr5TzaqcqHc','','###  Real Numbers and Their Decimal Expansions  \nWe study how real numbers can be expressed in **decimal form** – some terminate while others continue infinitely.\n',6,2,NULL),(11,'Terminating & Non-Terminating Decimals','video','https://www.youtube.com/live/IQE9OMTYy_A?si=4Eurl0C-C5eVkWhR&t=185','','\nHere, we prove that **rational numbers have either terminating or non-terminating recurring decimal expansions** and solve related problems.\n',7,2,NULL),(12,'Irrational Numbers','video','https://youtu.be/lS58KEo3x60?si=sr5KBLKxtFPYMHDp','','###  Irrational Numbers  \nWe learn about **irrational numbers** like √2, √3, π, etc., which cannot be expressed as fractions and have **non-terminating, non-repeating decimals**.',8,2,NULL),(13,'Exercise 1.4 Problems and Concepts','video','    https://youtu.be/XY82_7dfSc0?si=qXA3Im0pZS34sACX','','### Exercise 1.4  \nPractice problems on **decimal expansions of rational and irrational numbers**.',9,2,NULL),(14,'Exponential\'s Revisted','video','    https://youtu.be/A4w0cHnxR-8?si=gxpustOE0MUZwW91https://youtu.be/A4w0cHnxR-8?si=gxpustOE0MUZwW','','###  Exponentials Revisited  \nThis section revises the **laws of exponents** and applies them to simplify numbers.',10,2,NULL),(15,'Logarithms','video','   https://youtu.be/oPtqAnh8c78?si=4Cj4eJHR_o0PdllH','','###  Logarithms  \nWe study **logarithms**, which are another way of writing exponents.  \nExample: log₁₀(100) = 2 because 10² = 100.\n',11,2,NULL),(16,'Properties  Logarithmic & Exercise 1.5 Problems and Concepts','video',' https://youtu.be/gjPN667LfYs?si=DjNch_Tu4NfLm5m_','','### Properties of Logarithms & Exercise 1.5  \nImportant **logarithmic properties** (product, quotient, and power rules) are explained with practice problems.  ',12,2,NULL),(63,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Social Science Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Social Science in this comprehensive lesson','',1,32,NULL),(64,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Social Science Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Social Science in this comprehensive lesson','',2,32,NULL),(65,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Social Science Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Social Science in this comprehensive lesson','',1,33,NULL),(66,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Social Science Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Social Science in this comprehensive lesson','',2,33,NULL),(67,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Social Science Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Social Science in this comprehensive lesson','',1,34,NULL),(68,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Social Science Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Social Science in this comprehensive lesson','',2,34,NULL),(69,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Social Science Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Social Science in this comprehensive lesson','',1,35,NULL),(70,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Social Science Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Social Science in this comprehensive lesson','',2,35,NULL),(71,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Mathematics Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',1,36,NULL),(72,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Mathematics Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',2,36,NULL),(73,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Mathematics Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',1,37,NULL),(74,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Mathematics Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',2,37,NULL),(75,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Mathematics Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',1,38,NULL),(76,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Mathematics Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',2,38,NULL),(77,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Mathematics Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',1,39,NULL),(78,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Mathematics Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',2,39,NULL),(79,'Lesson 1: Introduction to Mathematics Part 1','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',1,40,NULL),(80,'Lesson 2: Introduction to Mathematics Part 2','video','','Learn the basics of Mathematics in this comprehensive lesson','',2,40,NULL),(81,'Introduction to Sets','video','https://youtu.be/GJYPnCUkQik?si=MC38-069C63jEZRN','','### 1. Introduction to Sets\nThis lesson provides a foundational understanding of what **sets** are, why they are important in mathematics, and the basic terminology used to describe them.  \n- A **set** is a well-defined collection of distinct objects.  \n- Examples: {1, 2, 3}, {a, e, i, o, u}, {red, blue, green}.  \n',0,32,NULL),(82,'Roster Form & Set Builder Form','video','https://youtu.be/dnSUf5r6Ojc?si=ykxcuWF1WKHfY-Zg','','###  Roster Form & Set Builder Form\nThis topic explains the two primary methods for representing sets:  \n- **Roster Form**: Listing all the elements of a set explicitly.  \n  Example: The set of vowels = {a, e, i, o, u}.  \n- **Set Builder Form**: Describing the properties of elements in a set.  \n  Example: The set of vowels = {x | x is a vowel in the English alphabet}.  \n',1,32,NULL),(83,'Exercise 2.1  Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/rBSuFShv2y0?si=974v0N-XAO_1fBr9','','### Exercise 2.1\nPractice problems based on **Introduction to Sets** and **Roster/Set Builder Forms**.  \n- Identify sets and represent them in both forms.  \n- Convert between roster and set-builder forms. ',2,32,NULL),(84,'Sets (General Explanation)','video','https://youtu.be/Ucae-R_CwHE?si=WPQeyan9fTxxGeyDex','','###  Sets (General Explanation)\nA broader explanation of sets with more examples:  \n- Empty Set (∅)  \n- Singleton Set  \n- Finite & Infinite Sets  \n- Equal & Equivalent Sets  \n- Subsets and Supersets  \n',3,32,NULL),(85,'Exercise 2.2   Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/hmHrEiAK6cI?si=bumObldSTnA9-ffw','','###  Exercise 2.2\nProblems based on the **general explanation of sets**:  \n- Identify different types of sets.  \n- Work with examples of subsets, supersets, and equal sets.  \n\n---',4,32,NULL),(86,'Exercise 2.3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/Jgn5xcXynD8?si=QMozGHe_7LNAEY9s','','###  Exercise 2.3\nChallenging problems to strengthen understanding:  \n- Applying set concepts in word problems.  \n- Real-life examples of sets and their usage.  ',5,32,NULL),(87,'Finite and Infinite Sets','reading','','','# Finite and Infinite Sets – Class 10 (TS State Board)\n\n## 1. Introduction\nIn mathematics, sets are collections of well-defined objects.  \nDepending on the number of elements they contain, sets can be classified as **finite** or **infinite**.  \n\n---\n\n## 2. Finite Sets\n- **Definition:**  \n  A set is said to be **finite** if the number of elements in the set can be counted and is a natural number (0, 1, 2, …).  \n\n- **Examples:**  \n  1. \\( A = \\{2, 4, 6, 8, 10\\} \\) → 5 elements → finite.  \n  2. The set of vowels in the English alphabet \\( V = \\{a, e, i, o, u\\} \\).  \n  3. The set of students in your class.  \n\n- **Key Point:**  \n  A finite set can also be an **empty set (Ø)** because it has 0 elements (which is countable).  \n\n---\n\n## 3. Infinite Sets\n- **Definition:**  \n  A set is said to be **infinite** if the number of elements in the set cannot be counted, i.e., it goes on endlessly.  \n\n- **Examples:**  \n  1. \\( N = \\{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, …\\} \\) (set of natural numbers).  \n  2. The set of all real numbers between 0 and 1.  \n  3. The set of multiples of 5 → \\( \\{5, 10, 15, 20, …\\} \\).  \n\n- **Key Point:**  \n  Infinite sets never end. They cannot be expressed by simply writing all elements. Instead, we use **“…” (three dots)** or set-builder notation.  \n\n---\n\n## 4. Difference Between Finite and Infinite Sets\n\n| Finite Set | Infinite Set |\n|------------|--------------|\n| Has a definite number of elements. | Has unlimited, endless elements. |\n| Can be counted. | Cannot be completely counted. |\n| Example: {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}. | Example: {1, 2, 3, …}. |\n| May even have 0 elements (empty set). | Always non-empty. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Key Points for Exam\n- **Finite set:** Countable number of elements.  \n- **Infinite set:** Endless number of elements.  \n- All subsets of a finite set are also finite.  \n- Subsets of an infinite set can be either finite or infinite.  \n',6,32,NULL),(88,'Exercise 2.4 Problems and Concepts','reading','','','# 📢 Update Notice\n\nSome exercise problems do not have solutions available right now.  \nWe are working hard to prepare and provide the solutions as soon as possible.  \n\n✅ Please stay tuned — we will update the missing solutions very soon.  \nThank you for your patience and understanding. 🙏\n',7,32,NULL),(89,'Introduction','video','https://youtu.be/qO7cmcuS5P0?si=MRmhQ5T5k-7CQcrg','','This lesson introduces the **core concepts of polynomials**:  \n- A **polynomial** is an algebraic expression consisting of variables and coefficients, combined using only addition, subtraction, and multiplication, with variables raised to whole number powers.  \n  Example: \\( 3x^3 + 2x^2 - 5x + 7 \\).  \n\n- The **degree of a polynomial** is the highest power of the variable in the expression.  \n  Example: In \\( 4x^4 + 3x^2 + 7 \\), the degree is 4.  \n\n- The **value of a polynomial** is found by substituting a specific number in place of the variable.  \n  Example: For \\( p(x) = x^2 + 1 \\), \\( p(2) = 2^2 + 1 = 5 \\).  \n\n- A **zero of a polynomial** is the value of the variable that makes the polynomial equal to zero.  \n  Example: For \\( p(x) = x^2 - 4 \\), the zeros are \\( x = 2 \\) and \\( x = -2 \\).  \n\nThis topic lays the foundation for working with polynomials throughout the chapter.  ',0,33,NULL),(90,'What are Polynomials and Degree of a Polynomial ,Value of A Polynomial ,Zero  of A Polynomial','video','https://youtu.be/JMBur2S8inw?si=bUentJ7EMM1r1PA6&t=110','','This lesson introduces the core concepts of polynomials:\n\nA polynomial is an algebraic expression consisting of variables and coefficients, combined using only addition, subtraction, and multiplication, with variables raised to whole number powers.\nExample: \n3\n𝑥\n3\n+\n2\n𝑥\n2\n−\n5\n𝑥\n+\n7\n3x\n3\n+2x\n2\n−5x+7.\n\nThe degree of a polynomial is the highest power of the variable in the expression.\nExample: In \n4\n𝑥\n4\n+\n3\n𝑥\n2\n+\n7\n4x\n4\n+3x\n2\n+7, the degree is 4.\n\nThe value of a polynomial is found by substituting a specific number in place of the variable.\nExample: For \n𝑝\n(\n𝑥\n)\n=\n𝑥\n2\n+\n1\np(x)=x\n2\n+1, \n𝑝\n(\n2\n)\n=\n2\n2\n+\n1\n=\n5\np(2)=2\n2\n+1=5.\n\nA zero of a polynomial is the value of the variable that makes the polynomial equal to zero.\nExample: For \n𝑝\n(\n𝑥\n)\n=\n𝑥\n2\n−\n4\np(x)=x\n2\n−4, the zeros are \n𝑥\n=\n2\nx=2 and \n𝑥\n=\n−\n2\nx=−2.\n\nThis topic lays the foundation for working with polynomials throughout the chapter.',1,33,NULL),(91,'Exercise 3.1: 1 and 2 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/pYJC-m_km8M?si=WIWYUN_lCxmhmV9g','','This exercise tests the **basic concepts of polynomials**.  \n- It includes problems on identifying polynomials, finding their degrees, and evaluating polynomials for given values.  \n- These problems strengthen the fundamental understanding of the topic',2,33,NULL),(92,'Exercise 3.1: 3 , 4 and 5 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/dyPb_2pKX00?si=Q2m8IjSwYrd9QK26','','This exercise tests the **basic concepts of polynomials**.  \n- It includes problems on identifying polynomials, finding their degrees, and evaluating polynomials for given values.  \n- These problems strengthen the fundamental understanding of the topic',3,33,NULL),(93,'Working With Polynomials ,Geometrical Meaning ,Graphical Representation of Quadratic Polynomial','video','https://youtu.be/9BmpoN7jQj8?si=AeEy-envl7XBYOV0','','This lesson explains how the **zeroes of a quadratic polynomial** are related to the graph of the polynomial.  \n- By plotting quadratic curves on the coordinate plane, you will understand the geometrical meaning of zeroes.  \n- The points where the graph cuts the x-axis represent the zeroes of the polynomial. ',4,33,NULL),(94,'Exercise 3.2 : 1 and 2 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/9BmpoN7jQj8?si=8ieeE9BAqiuGd7n0','','This exercise is based on the **graphical representation of polynomials**.  \n- It includes problems where you analyze quadratic graphs, interpret zeroes, and understand their meaning geometrically.  \n- Some problems are still pending, but the available ones provide strong practice in visualization.  \n',5,33,NULL),(95,'Exercise 3.2 : 3 , 4 and 5 Problems and Concepts','reading','','','# 📢 Update Notice\n\nSome exercise problems do not have solutions available right now.  \nWe are working hard to prepare and provide the solutions as soon as possible.  \n\n✅ Please stay tuned — we will update the missing solutions very soon.  \nThank you for your patience and understanding. 🙏\n',6,33,NULL),(96,'Realtionship Between Zeroes and Coefficients of A Polynomial','video','https://youtu.be/uKs5zLPNpKo?si=Wnq3qtxZMRSnRipY','','This exercise helps you practice problems on the **relationship between zeroes and coefficients**.  \n- It includes verifying relationships, applying formulas, and solving problems for quadratic and cubic polynomials.  \n\n\n\n',7,33,NULL),(97,'Exercise 3.3: 1 Problem and Concept','video','https://youtu.be/Rpmr-Qe5QGs?si=6_mod-7yRHTimJtW','','This exercise applies the **division algorithm** through practice problems.  \n- It includes dividing polynomials, finding quotients and remainders, and verifying the division algorithm step by step.  ',8,33,NULL),(98,'Exercise 3.3: 2 and 3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/aVDohv79HzE?si=56BJH_LDOPI08h84','','This exercise applies the **division algorithm** through practice problems.  \n- It includes dividing polynomials, finding quotients and remainders, and verifying the division algorithm step by step.  ',9,33,NULL),(99,'Exercise 3.3: 4 Problem and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/kK6hnkm-Jec?si=f0vcsuSMRVXjbrOs','','This exercise applies the **division algorithm** through practice problems.  \n- It includes dividing polynomials, finding quotients and remainders, and verifying the division algorithm step by step.  ',10,33,NULL),(100,'Divison Algorithm for Polynomials','video','https://youtu.be/aOYn4oPTVxA?si=3sh941TVg9u9ROV3','','### Division Algorithm for Polynomials\nThis lesson explains the **division algorithm for polynomials**.  \n- The algorithm states that any polynomial can be expressed as:  \n  \\[\n  p(x) = (x - a) \\cdot q(x) + r(x)\n  \\]  \n  where \\( p(x) \\) is the dividend, \\( q(x) \\) is the quotient, and \\( r(x) \\) is the remainder.  \n- This is a powerful tool for simplifying and solving polynomial problems.  \n',11,33,NULL),(101,'Exercise 3.4: 1 Problem and Concept','video','https://youtu.be/UgcX1bltiTI?si=v5mVnpcMwl9prssV','','',12,33,NULL),(102,'Exercise 3.4: 2 Problem and Concept','video','https://youtu.be/f_ZnqQGMFOo?si=EBPBtzA_dbMNuEqlv','','',13,33,NULL),(103,'Exercise 3.4: 3 and 5 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/lro8yl2vKD4?si=F9W3Lqjj7W5-TA7W','','',14,33,NULL),(104,'Exercise 3.4: 5 Problem and Concept','video','https://youtu.be/x4hb6qt_PyE?si=BukycVGMRi-x-69I','','',15,33,NULL),(105,'Introducation','video','https://youtu.be/5Y57mdzCJcY?si=05wmwAuxrPQCeFRW','','',0,34,NULL),(106,'Exercise-4.1','video','https://youtu.be/pGjLWGxQ24E?si=C-9jjZYUGxxvOTBr ','','',1,34,NULL),(107,'Substitution method','video','https://youtu.be/NT1sFzsHVrQ?si=jIYNA9m186HchPCu','','',2,34,NULL),(108,'Elimination  method:','video','https://youtu.be/TeNRE_1eMvs?si=uRgdQBna9rMN-PWL','','',3,34,NULL),(109,'Exercise-4.2: 1 , 2 and 3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/jGeFyz5ITTQ?si=XqU7jhFxNn_Nn_O4','','',4,34,NULL),(110,'Exercise-4.2: 4 , 5 and 6 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/h_qcpM0SPQo?si=ggnZgjFVT8npQf0G','','',5,34,NULL),(111,'Exercise-4.2: 7 , 8 ,9 and 10 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/z_JYHuze7p4?si=ZyYYJTFv49nNVfG1','','',6,34,NULL),(112,'Equations Reducible to A Pair of Linear Equations in Two','reading','','','## 4.4 Equations Reducible to a Pair of Linear Equations in Two Variables\n\nSometimes equations look non-linear (like fractions with x or y in the denominator),  \nbut we can change them into **linear equations** by a smart substitution.\n\n---\n\n### Example 12:\nSolve:\n\n2/x + 3/y = 13  \n5/x - 4/y = -2\n\n**Step 1: Substitution**  \nLet p = 1/x and q = 1/y  \n\nThen the equations become linear:  \n\n2p + 3q = 13  \n5p - 4q = -2\n\n---\n\n**Step 2: Solve by elimination**  \n\n- Coefficients of q are 3 and -4 → LCM = 12  \n- Multiply first equation by 4: 8p + 12q = 52  \n- Multiply second equation by 3: 15p - 12q = -6  \n\nAdd both equations:  \n8p + 12q + 15p - 12q = 52 + (-6)  \n23p = 46 → p = 2  \n\nSubstitute p = 2 into first equation:  \n2*2 + 3q = 13  \n4 + 3q = 13 → 3q = 9 → q = 3  \n\nBack-substitute to get x and y:  \nx = 1/p = 1/2  \ny = 1/q = 1/3  \n\n**Answer:** x = 1/2, y = 1/3\n\n---\n\n### Example 13:\nKavitha wants to build 2 more rooms.  \n\nGiven:  \n- 6 men + 8 women can finish the work in 10 days  \n- 26 women can finish the work\n',7,34,NULL),(113,'Exercise 4.3 : 1,2  Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/exuy446r9Rs?si=jbbESvz-O8tArWYb','','',8,34,NULL),(114,'Exercise 4.3 : 3,4  Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/082IF8jKE1Q?si=_M-wFQClRI8nNqNA','','',