YevpYq-uX','','',0,39,NULL),(181,'Exercise-9.1: Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/LfRxUtFOxfI?si=XwPZw-mzISGWY4OJ','','',1,39,NULL),(182,'Number of Tangents to Circle   from AnyPoint','reading','','','# Number of Tangents to a Circle from Any Point\n\n## Introduction\nA **tangent** to a circle is a straight line that touches the circle at exactly **one point**.  \nDepending on the position of a point relative to the circle, the number of tangents that can be drawn varies.\n\n---\n\n## Cases of Tangents\n\n### Case (i): Point inside the circle\n- If a point lies **inside the circle**, **no tangent** can be drawn to the circle through this point.  \n- Any line through the point will cut the circle at **two points**, thus forming a **secant** instead of a tangent.\n\n### Case (ii): Point on the circle\n- If a point lies **on the circle**, **exactly one tangent** can be drawn through this point.  \n- This tangent touches the circle at **only that single point**.\n\n### Case (iii): Point outside the circle\n- If a point lies **outside the circle**, **exactly two tangents** can be drawn from the point to the circle.  \n- These tangents touch the circle at **two distinct points**.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n| Position of Point      | Number of Tangents |\n|-------------------------|---------------------|\n| Point inside the circle | **0** tangents     |\n| Point on the circle     | **1** tangent      |\n| Point outside the circle| **2** tangents     |\n\n---\n\n## Theorem 9.2  \n**The lengths of tangents drawn from an external point to a circle are equal.**\n\n### Given:\n- A circle with centre **O**.\n- **P** is a point outside the circle.\n- **PA** and **PB** are tangents to the circle.\n\n### To Prove:\n- **PA = PB**\n\n### Proof:\n1. Join **O** to **A**, **B**, and **P**.  \n2. ∠OAP = ∠OBP = 90° (since **radius ⟂ tangent**).  \n3. In right triangles **OAP** and **OBP**:\n   - **OA = OB** (radii of the circle)\n   - **OP = OP** (common side)\n   - ∠OAP = ∠OBP = 90°\n4. By **RHS congruence criterion**:  \n   **ΔOAP ≅ ΔOBP**\n5. Therefore, **PA = PB** (by CPCT).  \n\nHence proved.\n\n---\n\n## Key Points to Remember\n1. A tangent touches a circle at **only one point**.  \n2. **No tangent** can be drawn from a point **inside** the circle.  \n3. **Exactly one tangent** can be drawn at a point **on** the circle.  \n4. **Exactly two tangents** can be drawn from a point **outside** the circle.  \n5. Tangents drawn from an **external point** to a circle are always **equal in length**.\n',2,39,NULL),(183,'Construction of Tangents to A Circles from An External Point','video','https://youtu.be/O9v1XqW4m_U?si=IyD1NMPMNxqBItaG','','',3,39,NULL),(184,'Exercise 9.2: 1,2 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/i3AvM-d-y3g?si=hMaaAiDAB-D-h9Cv','','',4,39,NULL),(185,'Exercise 9.2: 6 to 9 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/Y8awAoBX2jE?si=-8O_3VptTCCAuIui','','',5,39,NULL),(186,'Segment of A Circle By A Secant','video','https://youtu.be/aJ4vWs81ccQ?si=f0TMcrs6BgxqMZsf','','',6,39,NULL),(187,'Finding Area of Segment of A Circle:','reading','','','# Finding the Area of a Segment of a Circle\n\n## Introduction\nA **segment of a circle** is the part of the circle bounded by a **chord** (a straight line joining two points on the circle) and the **arc** between those two points.  \nTo find the area of a segment, we calculate the **area of the sector** and subtract the **area of the triangle** formed by the two radii and the chord.\n\n---\n\n## Formula\nLet:\n- **r** = radius of the circle  \n- **θ** = central angle of the sector in degrees  \n\nThe formula for the area of a segment is:\n\n**Area of Segment**  \n= **Area of Sector** – **Area of Triangle**\n\nWhere,\n- **Area of Sector** = (θ / 360) × π × r²  \n- **Area of Triangle** = (1/2) × r² × sin θ  \n\nSo,\n**Area of Segment** = (θ / 360) × π × r² – (1/2) × r² × sin θ\n\n---\n\n## Steps to Find the Area of a Segment\n1. Find the **radius (r)** of the circle.  \n2. Measure the **central angle (θ)** in degrees (or convert to radians if you are using a calculator that needs radians).  \n3. Calculate the **area of the sector** using the formula (θ / 360) × π × r².  \n4. Calculate the **area of the triangle** using the formula (1/2) × r² × sin θ.  \n5. Subtract the triangle’s area from the sector’s area to get the **area of the segment**.\n\n---\n\n## Key Points\n- If you need the **minor segment** (the smaller region), use the given angle θ directly.  \n- For the **major segment** (the larger region), subtract the area of the minor segment from the total area of the circle:  \n  Area of Major Segment = π × r² – Area of Minor Segment  \n- Make sure θ is in **radians** if your calculator’s sine function uses radians.\n\n---\n\n## Example\nFind the area of a minor segment of a circle with a radius of **7 cm** if the central angle is **60°**.\n\n- **Area of Sector** = (60 / 360) × π × 7²  \n  = (1 / 6) × π × 49  \n  = (49π / 6) cm²  \n\n- **Area of Triangle** = (1/2) × 7² × sin 60°  \n  = (1/2) × 49 × (√3 / 2)  \n  = (49√3 / 4) cm²  \n\n- **Area of Segment** = Area of Sector – Area of Triangle  \n  = (49π / 6) – (49√3 / 4) cm²\n',7,39,NULL),(188,'Exercise 9.3: 1 to 4 Problem and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/I2jqNJ8W81A?si=6OMpOBwEDCnCgq9w','','',8,39,NULL),(189,'Exercise 9.3: 5 to 7 Problem and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/NRzw8-UOnqg?si=t82FVYRLj-n6bLXb','','',9,39,NULL),(190,'Introduction','reading','','','## Mensuration – Surface Area and Volume of Solid Shapes\n\nMensuration deals with the **surface area** and **volume** of 3-dimensional (solid) shapes.\n\n---\n\n### Applications\n- **Paint required** → Surface Area  \n- **Boxes for grains** → Volume  \n- **Canvas for tent** → Surface Area  \n- **Water or Gas** → Volume  \n\n**Examples of Applications**\n1. Quantity of water in a bottle → **Volume**  \n2. Canvas needed for a tent → **Surface Area**  \n3. Number of bags that fit in a lorry → **Volume**  \n4. Amount of gas in a cylinder → **Volume**  \n5. Number of matchsticks in a box → **Volume**\n\n---\n\n### Important Formulas\n\n#### 1. **Cylinder**\n- Curved Surface Area (CSA) = **2 × π × r × h**\n- Total Surface Area (TSA) = **2 × π × r × h + 2 × π × r²**\n- Volume = **π × r² × h**\n\n#### 2. **Sphere**\n- Surface Area = **4 × π × r²**\n- Volume = **(4/3) × π × r³**\n\n#### 3. **Hemisphere**\n- Curved Surface Area (CSA) = **2 × π × r²**\n- Total Surface Area (T\n',0,40,NULL),(191,'Exercise 10.1: Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/Hwx4JLZXbX0?si=5HyMTTz0c9-iKtqa','','',1,40,NULL),(192,'Suface  Area  of the Combination of Soild :','reading','','','# Surface Area of the Combination of Solids\n\n## Introduction\nMany real-life objects are made by **combining two or more basic solids** like cylinders, cones, spheres, hemispheres, or cubes.  \nTo find the **total surface area** of such a solid, we calculate the **curved surface areas (CSA)** or **lateral surface areas (LSA)** of each part and then add or subtract areas where the solids are joined.\n\n---\n\n## Key Idea\n- **Surface Area of Combination** = **Sum of exposed areas of all solids** – **Area of the parts that are joined together**  \n- Only the **outer surfaces** that can be seen or touched are included in the total surface area.\n\n---\n\n## Common Formulas\nLet’s recall the surface area formulas of basic solids:\n\n| Solid                  | Curved Surface Area (CSA) / Lateral Surface Area (LSA) | Total Surface Area (TSA) |\n|-------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| Cylinder (radius r, height h) | CSA = 2 × π × r × h | TSA = 2 × π × r × (r + h) |\n| Cone (radius r, slant height l) | CSA = π × r × l | TSA = π × r × (l + r) |\n| Sphere (radius r)       | – | TSA = 4 × π × r² |\n| Hemisphere (radius r)   | CSA = 2 × π × r² | TSA = 3 × π × r² |\n| Cube (side a)           | – | TSA = 6 × a² |\n| Cuboid (l × b × h)      | – | TSA = 2 × (lb + bh + hl) |\n\n---\n\n## Steps to Find Surface Area of a Combination\n1. **Identify each solid** in the combination (cylinder, cone, hemisphere, etc.).  \n2. Write down the **dimensions** (radius, height, slant height, side length, etc.).  \n3. Calculate the **curved or total surface area** of each solid as required.  \n4. **Add the exposed areas** of all the solids.  \n5. **Subtract the areas of the surfaces that are joined** (because these joined parts are not exposed).\n\n---\n\n## Example 1  \nA solid is made up of a **cylinder** of radius **r = 7 cm** and height **h = 10 cm** with a **hemisphere** of the same radius fixed on the top.  \nFind the total surface area.\n\n- **CSA of Cylinder** = 2 × π × r × h  \n  = 2 × π × 7 × 10 = 140π cm²\n\n- **CSA of Hemisphere** = 2 × π × r²  \n  = 2 × π × 7² = 98π cm²\n\n- **Base of Cylinder** is not exposed (it is kept on the ground), so we don’t include it.\n\n**Total Surface Area** = CSA of Cylinder + CSA of Hemisphere  \n= 140π + 98π = **238π cm²**\n\n---\n\n## Example 2  \nA toy is in the shape of a **cone** mounted on a **hemisphere**, both having a radius of **r**.  \nIf the slant height of the cone is **l**,  \nTotal Surface Area = CSA of Cone + CSA of Hemisphere  \n= (π × r × l) + (2 × π × r²)\n\n---\n\n## Key Points\n- Always check which surfaces are **exposed** and which are **hidden or joined**.  \n- If the base of a solid is exposed (like the bottom of a cylinder), include it in the **Total Surface Area**.  \n- Use the correct dimensions (radius, height, slant height) for each part of the solid.\n',2,40,NULL),(193,'Exercise 10.2: 1 to 3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/h4RIK3PpzAA?si=LiRCzHriyz3PxpUJ','','',3,40,NULL),(194,'Exercise 10.2: 4,5 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/LbJF_tn2EJk?si=zfYuYtB_xvD4y344','','',4,40,NULL),(195,'Exercise 10.2:  6, 7 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/s9OocE2zxJU?si=8Cuu2HvPrn0TrpzW','','',5,40,NULL),(196,'Volume of Combination of solid','reading','','','## Volume of Combination of Solids (10th Class)\n\nSometimes a 3D object is made by **joining two or more basic solids** like a cylinder, cone, hemisphere, or sphere.  \nTo find the **total volume**, we add or subtract the volumes of the individual solids.\n\n---\n\n### Key Idea\n**Total Volume = Sum of volumes of all parts (if joined)**  \n**OR**  \n**Total Volume = Volume of big solid – Volume of hollow part (if something is removed)**\n\n---\n\n### Common Combinations\n\n1. **Ice-cream Cone**  \n   - A cone with a hemisphere on top.  \n   - Volume = Volume of cone + Volume of hemisphere  \n   - Formula:\n     - Cone = (1/3) × π × r² × h\n     - Hemisphere = (2/3) × π × r³\n\n2. **Cylinder with Hemisphere on Top**  \n   - Example: A candle stand or a toy.  \n   - Volume = Volume of cylinder + Volume of hemisphere  \n   - Cylinder = π × r² × h  \n   - Hemisphere = (2/3) × π × r³\n\n3. **Cylinder with Cone on Top**  \n   - Example: A tent, a rocket, a pencil.  \n   - Volume = Volume of cylinder + Volume of cone  \n   - Cylinder = π × r² × h₁  \n   - Cone = (1/3) × π × r² × h₂\n\n4. **Sphere Carved out of a Cube**  \n   - A sphere is cut out from a cube.  \n   - Volume = Volume of cube – Volume of sphere  \n   - Cube = a³  \n   - Sphere = (4/3) × π × r³\n\n5. **Hollow Cylinder (like a pipe)**  \n   - Volume = π × h × (R² – r²)  \n     - R = outer radius  \n     - r = inner radius  \n     - h = height\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step Method\n\n1. **Identify the solids** inside the object (cylinder, cone, sphere, etc.).  \n2. **Write the formula** for each solid’s volume.  \n3. **Use same units** (all cm or all m).  \n4. Add or subtract the volumes as needed.\n\n---\n\n### Quick Example\nAn ice-cream cone has radius 7 cm, cone height 24 cm.\n\n- Volume of cone = (1/3) × π × 7² × 24  \n                  =\n',6,40,NULL),(197,'Exercise 10.3: 1 to 3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/X7rVXp6dlaY?si=STUOUefRotEdBYmu','','',7,40,NULL),(198,'Exercise 10.3: 4 and 5 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/iOz2XsrkKz0?si=q5xIr3TD2qumkyRQ','','',8,40,NULL),(199,'Conversion of Solid from One shape to Another','reading','','','\n\n# Surface Area of a Sphere\n\n## Introduction\nA **sphere** is a perfectly round three-dimensional solid where every point on the surface is at the same distance from its center.\n\nExamples include a ball, a globe, and a bubble.\n\nThe **surface area of a sphere** is the total area of its curved surface.\n\n---\n\n## Formula\nLet:\n\n- **r** = radius of the sphere\n\nThe formula for the surface area is:\n\n**Surface Area of Sphere** = 4 × π × r²\n\n---\n\n## Steps to Find the Surface Area\n1. Measure or find the **radius (r)** of the sphere.  \n2. Use the formula **4 × π × r²** to calculate the surface area.\n\n3. If the diameter **d** is given, remember that **r = d / 2**,\n\nso the formula becomes **Surface Area = π × d²**.\n\n---\n\n## Example\nFind the surface area of a sphere with a radius of **7 cm**.\n\n**Surface Area** = 4 × π × r²\n\n= 4 × π × 7²\n\n= 4 × π × 49\n\n= **196π cm²**\n\n(If π = 3.14, the answer is approximately **615.44 cm²**.)\n\n---\n\n## Key Points\n- The surface area of a sphere depends **only on the radius**.  \n- If the **diameter** is known, first divide by 2 to get the radius.  \n- Always use the same unit (cm, m, etc.) throughout the calculation.\n\n',9,40,NULL),(200,'Exercise 10.4 : 1 to 3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/rYlNyKsdJOs?si=0XwlzxO5e32bxv_Z','','',10,40,NULL),(201,'Exercise 10.4 : 4 to 6 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/LMi8XiGl9Pk?si=imHH8_UrPNV6_3kf','','',11,40,NULL),(202,'Exercise 10.4 : 7 and 8 Problems and Concepts','video','','','',12,40,NULL),(203,'Introducation','video','https://youtu.be/uFCaHFl_IG8?si=uuXmbVAlq-aHwF1J','','',0,41,NULL),(204,'Naming Side In Right Triangle:','reading','','','# Naming the Sides in a Right Triangle\n\n## Introduction\nIn a **right-angled triangle**, one of the angles is exactly **90°**.  \nThe three sides of the triangle are given specific names based on their position with respect to a chosen angle (other than the right angle).  \nCorrectly naming these sides is important for applying trigonometric ratios like sine, cosine, and tangent.\n\n---\n\n## Names of the Sides\n\nConsider a right triangle **ABC** where ∠B = 90°.  \nLet’s take angle **A** as the reference angle (other than the right angle).  \nThe sides are named as follows:\n\n1. **Hypotenuse**  \n   - The side **opposite the right angle**.  \n   - It is the **longest side** of the triangle.  \n   - In this example, **AC** is the hypotenuse.\n\n2. **Opposite Side** (also called **Perpendicular**)  \n   - The side **opposite to the reference angle**.  \n   - For angle A, the side opposite is **BC**.\n\n3. **Adjacent Side** (also called **Base**)  \n   - The side **next to the reference angle**, excluding the hypotenuse.  \n   - For angle A, the adjacent side is **AB**.\n\n---\n\n## Important Notes\n- The naming of **opposite** and **adjacent** sides **changes** depending on the chosen reference angle.  \n- The **hypotenuse** always remains the same because it is fixed opposite the right angle.\n\n---\n\n## Example\nIf angle **C** is the reference:\n- Hypotenuse = **AC** (remains the same)\n- Opposite side = **AB**\n- Adjacent side = **BC**\n\n---\n\n## Key Points to Remember\n1. **Hypotenuse** → Always opposite the 90° angle and is the\n',1,41,NULL),(205,'Exercise-11.1: 1 to 4 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/6vESvOjNP4U?si=FEtvQgdftL6MFwlZ','','',2,41,NULL),(206,'Exercise-11.1: 5 to 8 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/tUOF-kMMRNQ?si=uv12T4LgPhGvdmMo','','',3,41,NULL),(207,'Trigonometry  Ratios of Some Specific Angles','video','https://youtu.be/t7-LxMqLgyw?si=P231mX09aIl5zoC-','','',4,41,NULL),(208,'Exercise 11.2 : 1,2 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/AsvlpbWB_tw?si=-Vuz6vTeeQZ8BJgW','','',5,41,NULL),(209,'Exercise 11.2 : 3 to 7 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/InJc1Eu0Jlo?si=F1iFyQZkVrt0ECfT','','',6,41,NULL),(210,'Trigonometry Ratio of Complementary Angles:','video','https://youtu.be/ysadGrTZcpw?si=Kf_O_2Xhco4jImUR','','',7,41,NULL),(211,'Exercise 11.3: Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/pSDaw-MN1QU?si=-kXDJDUQ8HOuxeow','','',8,41,NULL),(212,'Trigonometric  Identities','video','https://youtu.be/jIIVvXx2Idk?si=ganGmwW8QNOrZ_Uc&t=780','','',9,41,NULL),(213,'exercise 11.4: 1 to 4 Problems  and  Concepts','video','  \"https://youtu.be/8hvp9Xhmiu8?si=Fb_VXv6tj29mYEcX\"','','',10,41,NULL),(214,'exercise 11.4:  5 to 19 Problems  and  Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/ZrxrqxrZqbo?si=lMiA5oGgNuAf0_UQ','','',11,41,NULL),(215,'Introducation','video','https://youtu.be/eR3n3nkn9sY?si=S_hUHyqxJ-R1O0ko','','',0,42,NULL),(216,'Exercise 12.1: 1 to 3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/UouHUuVVnps?si=QItz6QlJlgsK3koR','','',1,42,NULL),(217,'Exercise 12.1: 4 to 6 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/AyNKqkytUhs?si=7i8LVF6GvJ0DXJd7','','',2,42,NULL),(218,'Exercise 12.1: 7 to 10Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/fQ1umX4v0mE?si=qq6qne0WcaVo6YzQ','','',3,42,NULL),(219,'Exercise 12.2: 1,2 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/sQ2AmQMH01M?si=8uxCBejWsDhRmtLA','','',4,42,NULL),(220,'Exercise 12.2: 3 to 5 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/Q2bhbJFxTb8?si=825eOCVIN617ufZ-','','',5,42,NULL),(221,'Exercise 12.2: 6 , 7 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/i5kk1aRPj84?si=cyyFP71UPgDJuV8C','','',6,42,NULL),(222,'Exercise 12.2: 8 ,9 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/CWlHoIbG1Cc?si=ySXgfoCjwFTXKK0w','','',7,42,NULL),(223,'Introduction','video','https://youtu.be/P7fv0DkWDiQ?si=7ugT7UAnyWa31VmN','','',0,43,NULL),(224,'Exercise 13.1: 1 to 5 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/6Hd4mEUpNPU?si=IFStoWP7zyeLAxiM','','',1,43,NULL),(225,'Exercise 13.1: 6 to 9 Problems and Concepts','reading','','','# 📢 Update Notice\n\nSome exercise problems do not have solutions available right now.  \nWe are working hard to prepare and provide the solutions as soon as possible.  \n\n✅ Please stay tuned — we will update the missing solutions very soon.  \nThank you for your patience and understanding. 🙏',2,43,NULL),(226,'Exercise 13.2: 1 to 5 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/6Hd4mEUpNPU?si=IFStoWP7zyeLAxiM','','',3,43,NULL),(227,'Exercise 13.2: 6 to 10 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/kPZZNaAQzjI?si=o6KPCQtSSJ_MFoPi','','',4,43,NULL),(228,'Exercise 13.2: 10 to 15 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/aLmoHOhknCM?si=4SCVFpdUIKcTrhyd','','',5,43,NULL),(229,'Introduction','video','https://youtu.be/2tlqPVCxMGE?si=nOyLOEqXeFAZuFjh','','',0,44,NULL),(230,'Exercise14.1: 1 to 3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/3PuTpqKfrYU?si=QQ1JDh13XBSRN_mZ','','',1,44,NULL),(231,'Exercise14.1: 4 to 6 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/dCDuoxibYhA?si=53srR8Y7RGj6kgq4','','',2,44,NULL),(232,'Mode','video','https://youtu.be/AxOsZB0uEUs?si=yOGn_J4qUJXXZBCF','','',3,44,NULL),(233,'Exercise 14.2 : 1,2 Problem and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/SkYENxhHyhk?si=Z3xXSx4HqqqqDSIp','','',4,44,NULL),(234,'Exercise 14.2 : 3 to 6 Problem and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/HcHL3sojxB0?si=e2zK2HesVEJX4ZPL','','',5,44,NULL),(235,'Median of Group Data','video','https://youtu.be/tMRasiLK1W8?si=oJAsec7a10fJ5sts','','',6,44,NULL),(236,'Which Value of Central Tendency','reading','','','# Measures of Central Tendency\n\n## Introduction\n**Central tendency** is a statistical measure that identifies a single value as representative of the **center or middle** of a data set.  \nIt helps to summarize a large set of data with a single value that reflects the general trend of the data.  \nThe three main measures of central tendency are:\n\n1. **Mean**  \n2. **Median**  \n3. **Mode**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Mean (Arithmetic Mean)\n\n### Definition\nThe **mean** is the average of all the observations in a data set.  \n\n### Formula\nIf a data set has **n** observations: \\(x_1, x_2, x_3, ..., x_n\\),  \nthen the mean is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Mean} = \\frac{x_1 + x_2 + x_3 + ... + x_n}{n}\n\\]\n\n### Steps to Find the Mean\n1. Add all the observations in the data set.  \n2. Count the total number of observations (**n**).  \n3. Divide the sum by the total number of observations.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Median\n\n### Definition\nThe **median** is the **middle value** of a data set when the data is arranged in **ascending or descending order**.  \n- If the number of observations is **odd**, the median is the middle value.  \n- If the number of observations is **even**, the median is the **average of the two middle values**.\n\n### Steps to Find the Median\n1. Arrange the data in **increasing or decreasing order**.  \n2. Find the **middle observation(s)**.  \n3. Apply the rule for odd or even number of observations to get the median.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Mode\n\n### Definition\nThe **mode** is the observation that **occurs most frequently** in the data set.  \n- A data set can have **one mode (unimodal)**, **two modes (bimodal)**, or **more (multimodal)**.  \n- If all observations occur with the same frequency, there is **no mode**.\n\n### Steps to Find the Mode\n1. Count the **frequency** of each observation.  \n2. Identify the observation with the **highest frequency**.  \n3. That observation is the **mode**.\n\n---\n\n## Key Points to Remember\n1. **Mean** uses all observations and is affected by **extreme values** (outliers).  \n2. **Median** divides the data into **two equal halves** and is not affected by extreme values.  \n3. **Mode** shows the **most common observation** and may not exist in some data sets.  \n4. All three measures are used to **summarize data**, but the choice depends on the **nature of the data**.  \n\n---\n\n## Example\nData set: 5, 7, 8, 9, 12, 12, 15  \n\n- **Mean** = (5 + 7 + 8 + 9 + 12 + 12 + 15) / 7 = 68 / 7 ≈ 9.71  \n- **Median** = Middle value = 9  \n- **Mode** = Most frequent value = 12\n',7,44,NULL),(237,'Exercise 14.3 : 1 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/ZrScZb23NHw?si=eviZ9XpLYTJVEWHJ','','',8,44,NULL),(238,'Exercise 14.3 : 2 to 4 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/I9zutHxRJ54?si=-qgCDoCqPdjk_2ex','','',9,44,NULL),(239,'Exercise 14.3 : 5 to 7 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/gEbab_FnuKI?si=ByORZdF3B1QiV5KF','','',10,44,NULL),(240,'Graphical Representation of Cumlative Frequency Distribution','video','https://www.youtube.com/live/-Xi3CIcLLAE?si=0x5gOwRvarLGKd8M&t=10125','','',11,44,NULL),(241,'Exercise 14.4: 1 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/-Sr3iHYfyiI?si=RpfsLDILUuLUTivg','','',12,44,NULL),(242,'Exercise 14.4: 2,3 Problems and Concepts','video','https://youtu.be/3H9Vy001hqk?si=TlSN45NBgg6Hm1xG','','',13,44,NULL),(243,'What is Python? ','video','https://youtu.be/hEgO047GxaQ?si=UaKU0vWsLUy8wxA5','','',0,NULL,4),(244,'Installing Python & IDEs PyCharm','video','https://youtu.be/CScxy0294SE?si=8qL580IeFGre7CHK','','',1,NULL,4),(245,' First Python Program: Part-1 ','video','https://youtu.be/akcEaEH91gI?si=Rcab6wFufpIJNCqy','','',2,NULL,4),(246,' First Python Program: Part-2','video','https://youtu.be/DWgzHbglNIo?si=wnuuPnwwudK0CJ4','','',3,NULL,4),(247,' Comments in Python:','video','https://youtu.be/ECxZtt6n90E?si=EBAUhZktk55Kek2F','','',4,NULL,4),(248,'Basic of Python','quiz','','','',5,NULL,4),(249,'','video','','','',0,NULL,5),(250,'','video','','','',0,NULL,6),(251,'','video','','','',0,NULL,7),(252,'','video','','','',0,NULL,8),(253,'','video','','','',0,NULL,9),(254,'','video','','','',0,NULL,10),(255,'','video','','','',0,NULL,11);
